someone making an appeal to authority  asserts that an argument like their own  argument or someone else's is good or  bad or that a claim is true or false  because an authority or someone they're  claiming as an authority says it's true  or false or good or bad the suppose  adora T could be a person a book an  organization or any other source of  information and this can be done in a  totally proper way much of the time  there's absolutely nothing wrong with  doing this plenty of sources are actual  qualified authorities on whatever topic  but where you run into trouble and your  appeal to Authority becomes fallacious  is when there's good reason to think  that your source is not necessarily  reliable as a source of information on  the topic at hand let's consider this  conversation Mike told me Beethoven was  heavily influenced by Mozart so what  what is Mike know he has a PhD in  musicology this would probably make Mike  a reliable authority on this topic what  if the conversation went like this Mike  told me Beethoven was heavily influenced  by Mozart so what what does Mike know he  looked it up online this would not  necessarily qualify Mike as an authority  on the topic an appeal to Authority  assumes this type of implicit premise  almost anything that X says about Y is  probably true if we don't have good  reason to think that this premise is  true then it's a bad appeal to Authority  a fallacy and the ensuing argument  cannot be sound almost anything that  someone says about Beethoven's  influences who has looked it up online  is probably true Mike looked up  Beethoven's influences online Mike told  me Beethoven was heavily influenced by  Mozart  therefore Beethoven was probably heavily  influenced by Mozart we are relying on a  false premise but let's replace the  first premise with almost anything that  a PhD  musicologist says about Beethoven's in  Florence's is probably true now we're  working with true premises but notice  that I'm saying in my premise anything  or almost anything that XS about Y is  probably true what an expert on a topic  believes about that topic is not  necessarily true but we can say that it  is probably true if it's established  that the expert is reliable experts  don't necessarily decide what's true  they merely report on findings in order  to tell you what is probably true and if  they discover contradicting evidence  they'll report something else but  determining whether a sources and  authority can be tricky different people  and different audiences have different  assumptions about who's reliable on  which topics let's say we're talking  about a scientific expert making a  scientific assertion best practice in  determining the reliability of a  scientists assertion about something  scientific is recognizing whether his  assertion agrees with the vast consensus  of the scientific community because when  we're appealing to the authority of  science we shouldn't be appealing to  what one scientist thinks it doesn't  matter what they think we're appealing  to what science as an enterprise or  institution has to say about the matter  that's how science works the consensus  is meant to be what we appeal to if 3  percent of scientists say a and 97  percent of scientists say B you can't  say science tells us a you can say that  science is wrong about B and give us  your own reasons for making that claim  or that science is unreliable in general  but that's a different matter altogether  therefore when appealing to science your  argument should sort of look like this  premise 1 the consensus of the  scientific community is Z premise 2 the  consensus of the scientific community is  the most reliable information available  conclusion according to the most  reliable information available Z is true  if you're rejecting the scientific  community's consensus your argument is  premise one the consensus of the  scientific community is Z premise two  the consensus of the scientific  community is not reliable conclusion  being we have no reason to think that Z  is true many people rejecting a  scientific consensus don't even realize  they're making this argument and often  don't realize that they have the burden  of proving premise two that the  scientific community is not reliable  they would have to prove that the  scientific community is unreliable on  many issues and proving that is an  uphill battle this miniseries has been  made possible in part by brilliant  you're watching this video so chances  are you have a genuine interest in logic  philosophy and problem-solving want to  further your mastery you should check  out this fascinating online learning  service with brilliant you can solve  hands-on puzzles and problems developed  by professors and expert professionals  from all over the world brush up on your  skills when it comes to logical  fallacies syllogisms deductive reasoning  game theory and other domains of  philosophy math and science quickly  become and all-around better thinker  sign up for free by going to brilliant  org slash critical thinking I've put a  link in the description under this video  and the first 200 people who visit  brilliant org slash critical thinking  will get 20% off an annual Premium  Membership visit brilliant org slash  critical thinking
 

